<TopicPage slug="subjects/economics/advanced/game-theory">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    Almost everything before this lesson studied choice against <em>nature</em>: in Supply and
    Demand, Elasticity and GDP &amp; National Accounts each decision-maker treats prices as
    given. This lesson switches the object of study to choice against another thinker. It
    rebuilds the strategic corners you have already met — oligopoly in Market Failure &amp;
    Externalities, the wage bargain in Unemployment &amp; Labour Markets, the
    lender-of-last-resort role in Monetary Policy &amp; Central Banks, saving and spending in
    Money and Inflation — as explicit games with payoffs and best responses.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>define a Nash equilibrium and explain why stability and collective benefit are different properties;</li>
    <li>model a bank run, a household bargain, a wage contract and a capacity choice as a game;</li>
    <li>explain why a signal separates types only when it is differentially costly;</li>
    <li>compute when repetition sustains cooperation, and say what the threatened punishment must be;</li>
    <li>read a payoff matrix off cost and revenue accounting, and predict how objectives change the game.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Most of economics studies choice against <em>nature</em>: a farmer bets on the weather, a
    consumer on prices she cannot move. Game theory studies choice against <em>strategists</em> —
    situations where my best move depends on what I expect you to do, and your best move depends
    on your expectation of me. Von Neumann and Morgenstern (1944) gave the subject its
    mathematics; John Nash (1950, Nobel 1994) gave it its central concept, the <strong>Nash
    equilibrium</strong>: a combination of strategies in which no player can do better by changing
    strategy <em>alone</em>. Stability, not fairness, is what an equilibrium promises — and the gap
    between the two is where this whole lesson lives. The microeconomic decision-makers unit is,
    underneath, a catalogue of these situations: banks face depositors, households negotiate
    internally, workers and firms set effort and wages, oligopolists set prices, and every one of
    them is playing against another thinking mind.
  </p>
  <p>
    The canonical example is the <strong>prisoner's dilemma</strong>: cooperate pays 3 to each
    player, mutual defection pays 1 each, but exploiting a cooperator pays 5 while being exploited
    pays 0. Defection is every player's best response no matter what the other does — the slider
    below makes that numeric — so both defect and earn 1 each when both could have earned 3.
    This gap between individual and collective rationality is the theory's deepest result: arms
    races, overfishing, price wars, tariff escalations and climate negotiations are all versions
    of it, which is why a tool built for poker became a general theory of social organisation.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the prisoner's dilemma, numerically — my best move depends on what I expect from you" widget="sliderLab" config={gameCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> slide the chance that the other player cooperates from 0
    up to 1 and watch both expected payoffs rise — yet the defect line never crosses below the
    cooperate line. That is what makes defection a <em>dominant</em> strategy here: no belief
    about the other player makes cooperation a best response in a one-shot dilemma. The
    escapes this lesson studies — deposit insurance, repeated-play punishments, binding
    commitments — all work the same way: they rewrite the payoffs until the lines finally
    cross.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> in one-shot dilemmas, individual rationality traps everyone; the
    institutions in the rest of the lesson are ways of re-drawing the payoff table. First
    stop: the most expensive coordination game in finance, the bank run.
  </p>

  <h2>Money and banking: the game inside a bank run</h2>
  <p>
    A bank performs <strong>maturity transformation</strong>: it takes deposits repayable tomorrow
    and lends them against houses and businesses repayable in twenty years. That service creates a
    coordination game. Make it concrete: a bank holds 20 million in liquid assets and has lent
    80 million against property worth 90 million if sold slowly — but only 50 million if dumped in
    a fire sale this week. If depositors stay calm, the bank pays out over time and every depositor
    is made whole. If enough depositors panic, the loan book must be dumped, the recovery rate
    collapses, and <em>running</em> becomes the rational move for everyone who is not already at
    the front of the queue. Two self-fulfilling equilibria, one bank. Diamond and Dybvig formalised
    this in 1983 (Nobel 2022), and the policy answer is game-theoretic: deposit insurance and the
    central bank acting as lender of last resort change the payoffs directly — if my deposit is
    guaranteed, panic no longer pays, and the bad equilibrium disappears. The run is not a moral
    failure of the crowd; it is a legitimate solution to a badly designed game.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "a Nash equilibrium is the best outcome for everyone." An
    equilibrium is merely <em>stable</em> — nobody wants to move alone. The bank run, like mutual
    defection in the dilemma, is a perfectly good equilibrium that ruins everybody. Economists
    therefore care about <em>which</em> equilibrium a situation selects, and about designing rules
    that delete the destructive ones.
  </p>

  <h2>Households: bargaining under one roof</h2>
  <p>
    The household is usually drawn as one decision-maker, but it is really a small coalition —
    savings versus spending, who takes which job, which city the family lives in — and coalition
    decisions are negotiated. The workhorse model is <strong>Nash bargaining</strong>: the agreed
    outcome divides the surplus created by cooperation, and each side's share depends on its
    <em>disagreement point</em> — what each would get alone. Suppose one partner earns 60,000 at
    home and the other is offered 80,000 abroad, where the first could earn only 55,000. Together
    they earn 135,000 by moving against 115,000 by staying: a 20,000 surplus to divide, and the
    split is decided by leverage, alternatives and patience — not by arithmetic alone. Who
    controls money inside the household also changes what is bought with it. Esther Duflo's study
    of South Africa's old-age pension (2003) found that when the pension arrived in a
    grandmother's hands rather than a grandfather's, girls' nutritional status improved — the
    same money, differently placed, different outcomes. Unitary household models miss exactly
    this, which is why the bargaining view reshaped development policy toward paying transfers
    to mothers.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> inside the household, the disagreement point and the placement of
    money — not arithmetic alone — decide how the surplus divides. Employment is the same
    problem with a trust twist: the firm pays first and observes effort only later.
  </p>

  <h2>Workers: wages, effort and signalling</h2>
  <p>
    The wage bargain is a game in three acts. First, <strong>effort</strong>: an employer cannot
    perfectly observe a worker's diligence, so employment is a trust game — the firm pays today
    for effort it will see later. <strong>Efficiency-wage theory</strong> (Shapiro and Stiglitz,
    1984) explains why firms deliberately pay above the market-clearing wage: with a job worth
    keeping, the cost of being caught shirking rises, so effort rises — and the resulting
    unemployment is itself the discipline, the economic price of "the sack". Second,
    <strong>bargaining</strong>: where a union negotiates, the wage splits the surplus from
    cooperation, and the disagreement point (strike versus lockout) decides the split — which is
    why strike funds and replacement-worker laws move wages without anyone touching
    productivity. Third, <strong>signalling</strong>: Spence (1973, Nobel 2001) showed education
    can be rational even if it adds no skill, purely because it is <em>cheaper for able people to
    obtain</em>. Run the numbers: a degree costs a high-ability worker 20,000 in effort and fees,
    a low-ability worker 60,000; employers pay 75,000 for degree-holders and 50,000 without. The
    premium is 25,000 — worth it for the high type (25,000 gained, 20,000 spent) and not the low
    type (25,000 gained, 60,000 spent), so only the high type invests, and the separation is
    self-enforcing. The signal works because it is differentially costly; make degrees free and
    effortless and the whole equilibrium collapses.
  </p>

  <h2>Firms and production: the capacity game</h2>
  <p>
    When two firms choose output in the same market, each one's profit depends on the other's
    capacity — the classic <strong>Cournot</strong> game (1838). Two airlines fly one route;
    demand gives price in thousands of pounds as P = 100 − Q, where Q is total seats in hundreds,
    and marginal cost is 20. Airline 1's best response to any Q₂ is to fly half the distance
    between zero and the leftover market: q₁ = (80 − Q₂)/2, and symmetrically for the rival.
    Solving the two best responses together gives each airline 26.7 hundred seats, a price of
    46.7, and profit of about 713 per firm. Compare the counterfactuals: a monopolist would fly
    40 hundred seats, charge 60 and earn 1,600; price-taking competition would run the price down
    to 20 and profit to zero. Oligopoly sits between — collusive monopoly profit is available
    <em>if</em> they can coordinate, which is the next section's problem. Notice the method: there
    is no "the" answer until you model what each side expects the other to do. Commitment is the
    strategic weapon here — signing a non-cancellable aircraft order sinks cost into capacity and
    converts "I could cut output" into "I have already bought the planes", moving the rival's
    best response before the game even starts.
  </p>

  <h2>Costs, revenue and objectives: building the payoff matrix</h2>
  <p>
    Game theory is silent until someone fills in the payoffs, and payoffs come from the cost and
    revenue accounting the firm-lessons teach. Consider the <strong>Bertrand</strong> price game:
    two petrol stations at one crossroads, marginal cost 1.00 per litre, 1,000 daily customers
    split evenly while prices match. At 1.40 each sells 500 litres at a 0.40 margin — 200 a day
    each. But undercutting to 1.39 takes the whole forecourt: 1,000 × 0.39 = 390. The rival
    undercuts back to 1.38, and the spiral ends only where undercutting stops paying — at 1.00,
    zero margin for both. This is the Bertrand paradox: with an identical product, <em>two</em>
    competitors can deliver the competitive outcome, which is why real duopolists so
    desperately differentiate brands, locations and loyalty schemes — differentiation is the
    escape hatch from the paradox. Objectives shift the matrix too: a satisficing firm (Simon),
    a revenue-maximising empire-builder (Baumol) or a state-owned enterprise playing for
    employment all play different games on identical costs — and a "rational" rival models that
    objective, not textbook profit-maximisation.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "game theory assumes everyone is selfish, so it cannot explain
    fairness, love or cooperation." The theory takes <em>payoffs</em> as given — whatever the
    player values, including other people's welfare — and studies how interdependent choices
    interact. A mother maximising her children's welfare and a firm maximising profit are equally
    game-theoretic; what the theory predicts is strategy, and what it cannot do is tell you what
    people should want.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> payoffs come from cost and revenue accounting, and objectives —
    profit, revenue, employment — quietly rewrite the matrix. The last section sorts the whole
    zoo of market structures by how much genuine strategy each one contains.
  </p>

  <h2>Types of markets: oligopoly is a game, the rest are not (much)</h2>
  <p>
    Market structure is really a taxonomy of strategic richness. <strong>Perfect competition</strong>
    is strategically empty: each farmer is a price-taker, so there is nothing to out-think — only
    arithmetic. <strong>Monopoly</strong> is strategy-free too: no rival, just optimisation against
    a demand curve. <strong>Oligopoly</strong> is the genuinely strategic case, and everything
    above — capacity games, price wars, kinked-demand intuition — is its anatomy. The deepest
    oligopoly result is that <em>repetition</em> changes the game. A cartel agreement is a
    prisoner's dilemma played over and over: each round, cheating on your quota buys a one-off
    gain (say 6 instead of the cooperative 5) but triggers a price war worth 1 per round forever
    after. Grim-trigger arithmetic: cooperation is worth 5 per round indefinitely; cheating is
    worth 6 once, then 1 per round. Cooperation survives exactly when the firm weighs the future
    enough — 5 ≥ 6 − 5δ solves to δ ≥ 0.2, so even impatient firms can sustain collusion. This is
    why OPEC quotas hold in most years and why competition law cares so much about
    <em>communication</em>: the agreement is usually implicit, sustained by the shadow of
    tomorrow's retaliation rather than by any signed document.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "economics is zero-sum — one side's gain is the other's loss."
    Poker is zero-sum; markets are not. A voluntary trade happens because both sides gain — that
    surplus is the entire subject of this discipline — and most games in this lesson (bargaining,
    banking, repeated oligopoly) have cooperative outcomes worth more to <em>both</em> players
    than the equilibrium. Treating every negotiation as a fixed pie is not realism; it is a
    strategic error that forfeits the surplus, and spotting it is why negotiators who "expand the
    pie" systematically beat those who fight over slices.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>In one sentence: what does a Nash equilibrium promise, and what does it explicitly not promise?</li>
    <li>Two petrol stations sell identical fuel at a marginal cost of 1.00 per litre. One prices at 1.20. Using the Bertrand logic from this lesson, what must the rival do, and where does the price spiral end?</li>
    <li>In a repeated cartel, cheating on your quota pays 6 once but triggers a price war worth 1 per round forever, while cooperating pays 5 per round. Why does cooperation become sustainable when firms weigh the future heavily — and what should competition law police as a result?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) It promises stability only: no player can do better by
    changing strategy <em>alone</em>. It does not promise fairness or collective benefit — the
    bank run and mutual defection are perfectly stable equilibria that ruin everyone, which is
    why economists care which equilibrium a situation selects. (2) The rival must undercut —
    taking the whole market at a slightly smaller margin beats sharing at 1.20 — and the spiral
    ends at marginal cost, 1.00, where undercutting stops paying. With an identical product,
    two competitors can deliver the competitive outcome; differentiation is the escape hatch.
    (3) A patient firm compares 5 per round indefinitely against 6 once and 1 per round after:
    once the future carries enough weight, the discounted punishment exceeds the one-off gain,
    and cooperation is self-enforcing. The glue is credible retaliation, so competition law
    should police the communication and coordination that keep the shadow of tomorrow's price
    war alive — not just signed agreements.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Game theory studies choice against strategists: my best move depends on my expectation of yours.</li>
    <li>A Nash equilibrium is stable, not good — bad equilibria are real, and institutions exist to delete them.</li>
    <li>In one-shot dilemmas defection is dominant; deposit insurance, repetition and commitment rewrite the payoffs.</li>
    <li>Bargaining outcomes turn on disagreement points — in households, unions and firms alike.</li>
    <li>Signalling separates types only when the signal is differentially costly; cheapen it and the equilibrium collapses.</li>
    <li>With identical products, Bertrand competition drives price to marginal cost; differentiation is the escape.</li>
    <li>Payoffs are whatever players value — the theory predicts strategy, never what people should want.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    Behavioural Economics relaxes the assumption that payoffs are given — its ultimatum-game
    evidence showed people pay real money to punish unfair splits. International Trade &amp;
    Comparative Advantage applies strategic interdependence between nations, where tariffs and
    retaliation form a dilemma played by governments, and Development Economics uses the
    mechanism-design view to build institutions that make good equilibria self-enforcing.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The formal core.</strong> Nash proved every finite game has at least one equilibrium
    in mixed strategies (1950); Selten's subgame perfection (Nobel 1994, shared) added
    credibility — threats that would not be carried out must be ignored; Harsanyi added games of
    incomplete information. The <strong>folk theorems</strong> of repeated games formalise the
    oligopoly lesson: with patient players, almost any outcome can be sustained by the right
    punishment scheme — which is less a comfort than a warning, since it means rules and
    institutions, not payoffs alone, choose our equilibria.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Mechanism design: game theory in reverse.</strong> Instead of analysing a given game,
    design the rules so that self-interest produces the outcome you want. Hurwicz, Maskin and
    Myerson earned the 2007 Nobel for its foundations; Milgrom and Wilson (Nobel 2020) designed
    the spectrum auctions that have allocated radio licences worth hundreds of billions;
    Alvin Roth's matching algorithms (Nobel 2012) pair medical residents with hospitals and
    kidney donors with patients, saving lives with theorems. The proof of concept was
    concrete: the first FCC spectrum auction built on the Milgrom–Wilson design collected
    617 million dollars in July 1994, and the broadband round that followed within a year
    raised about 7 billion — auction <em>form</em>, not just the asset, deciding revenue
    and efficiency.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The frontiers.</strong> Evolutionary game theory (Maynard Smith's evolutionarily
    stable strategy) drops the assumption of conscious calculation — strategies that pay better
    spread, which explains animal conflict and the persistence of fairness norms. Behavioural
    game theory documents how humans actually play: level-k reasoning, errors in beauty-contest
    games, and cooperation that decays in one-shot lab play but revives with reputation.
    Algorithmic game theory is the newest edge — regulators now ask whether pricing algorithms,
    each learning from the others, can slide into tacit collusion that no human ever agreed to.
    And the planetary dilemmas — carbon, fish, pathogens — remain the subject's hardest
    open case: Schelling's insight (Nobel 2005) that credible commitment and focal points can
    avert tragedy is the theory's most consequential unfinished argument.
  </p>

